WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.690
The Front Range exposes old Precambrian granite and metamorphic basement.

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:09.030
Sedimentary units rest above it and now dip eastward away from

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:13.750
the mountains. Resistant Dakota sandstone forms a hogback, while weaker fine-grained

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:15.810
layers erode into lower areas.

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:18.470
Map colors identify units and ages;

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.970
blue-colored old red rocks are not actually blue sandstone.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:28.310
The lowest sedimentary package contains reddish coarse conglomerate, breccia and sandstone.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:32.550
Streams and debris flows carried sediment from the Ancestral Rockies onto

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:37.530
older basement. Younger red units include coastal mudflat deposits and well-sorted

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:42.110
dune sandstone. The source records changing environments rather than one continuous

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:43.590
kind of red deposit.

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:48.610
The Jurassic Morrison Formation includes stream, wetland and lake deposits with

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:52.990
dinosaur evidence. Above it, the Dakota Formation contains shoreline and beach

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:56.010
features, including ripple marks, plant fossils and tracks.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:59.210
These units were deposited before their later tilting.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:03.110
A fossil track on a tilted bed records the old depositional

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:06.130
surface, not a dinosaur walking up the modern slope.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.910
Marine shale above the Dakota contains shells, shark teeth and fish

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:14.650
bones. Sandstone and limestone interbeds record smaller shifts.

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:17.830
Later sandstone, shale and coal mark a return to shoreline and

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:21.570
land environments. The sea advanced across the region and was later

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:25.490
overrun by deltas; compare the whole sequence to the paleogeographic maps.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:31.070
The youngest Green Mountain deposits include crystalline and volcanic clasts derived

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.150
from erosion of the rising Front Range.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:37.990
Deformation folded and faulted the buried sequence, and geological interpretation helped

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:42.810
locate petroleum at depth. Separate deposition, later tilting, erosion and fluid

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:46.310
trapping. Present elevation alone is not a complete history.
